00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:21.020
Now, automation has become
something of a looming threat

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:23.605
in recent years, but it
isn't really a new thing.

00:02:23.605 --> 00:02:25.770
In fact, the first American factory

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.940
started incorporating automated technology

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:29.987
almost 100 years ago.

00:02:29.987 --> 00:02:32.770
Much of America's post-war industrial boom

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:35.890
was fueled by the twin
efficiencies of Fordism

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:40.020
AKA that infamous assembly
line of humans and technology.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.070
But things got even more automated,

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:44.450
starting in the computer fueled 70s.

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:47.660
As early algorithms started to
take up much of the busy work

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:48.980
that humans used to do.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:52.200
Presumably with a giant
calculator and lots of legal pads.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.040
And since then, it's been
a pretty straight shot

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.840
towards intensified automation.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:58.900
So if this has been a
gradually growing process

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:00.311
for almost 100 years,

00:03:00.311 --> 00:03:03.640
why are people getting so
freaked out about it now?

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:04.894
The simplest answer is,

00:03:04.894 --> 00:03:08.060
technology is getting a
lot more sophisticated

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:13.060
while we stay pretty much the
same, and the stats are bleak.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:16.670
According to one, Oxford
study, 47% of jobs in the U.S.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:18.250
are at high risk of automation.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:19.300
And a more recent study

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.510
from the 38 country economic coalition,

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:26.510
the OECD, predicts that 14%
of OECD jobs are at high risk.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:28.442
And by some accounts, by 2035,

00:03:28.442 --> 00:03:32.450
two-thirds of American
jobs will be obsolete.

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:34.550
So just a future proof
this video real quick.

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:36.980
Hey fellow robots, once
you finish drinking

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.920
the blood of a human baby, be
sure to like and subscribe.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:41.360
Okay, we're back.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.010
Now reactions to this
predicted wave of automation

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:44.920
are varied.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:48.290
For some, these changes
are simply inevitable.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.560
Technology is always developed in ways

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.530
that shift the landscape of human labor.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:55.250
And if anything, this
is the natural outcome

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:56.540
of all of those commercials,

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.712
telling people to get degrees in STEM.

00:03:58.712 --> 00:04:00.670
For others, the future of automation

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:03.090
is something that should
be keeping us up at night,

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:05.840
because if 65% of workers are obsolete,

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.010
what are they gonna do?

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:09.450
For the automation doomers,

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.360
we're headed towards a dystopian future,

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:13.980
where a small percentage
of the well to do,

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.100
ride around in their self-driving cars

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.370
on the way to a sex spot orgy
at their friend Jeff's house.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:21.436
Obviously, yeah, it's Jeff Bezos.

00:04:21.436 --> 00:04:23.980
Meanwhile, the rest of us
scraped together nickels

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:25.700
from the sewer in hopes of buying

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:28.700
a freshly robot prepared Chipotle burrito.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:30.120
And yeah, in this dystopian future,

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:31.690
guac will still be extra.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:33.500
And these aren't fringe worries either.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:35.730
In the 2020 Democratic primary,

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.110
Andrew Yang's entire political platform

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:39.570
was centered around the necessity

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:42.440
of mitigating the economic
effects of automation.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:46.668
Specifically, he called for
UBI or universal basic income

00:04:46.668 --> 00:04:49.140
which we have a video about FYI.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.440
And UBI is a common feature
of discussions of automation.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:53.890
With some arguing for it,

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:56.240
as a sort of economic
harm reduction measure,

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:59.599
for those whose jobs will
be eradicated by algorithms.

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:02.830
Meanwhile, other more based theorist,

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:05.820
argue that UBI can be the
basis of a utopian future

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:09.010
where we all reap the
benefits of robot labor.

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:10.743
At this point, we need to ask ourselves,

00:05:10.743 --> 00:05:14.060
is paradigm shifting
automation really inevitable.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:18.080
And if so, is it going to
ruin your life in particular?

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:19.860
Well, maybe not.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.560
One school of thought on automation,

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:24.650
sometimes call accelerationism,
argues that full automation

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:26.190
isn't something to be feared

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.430
and something to be actively pursued.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:30.520
In their book, "Inventing the Future,"

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:32.737
Nick Srnicek and Alex Williams write,

00:05:32.737 --> 00:05:36.270
"Our first demand is for
a fully automated economy.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.530
Using the latest
technological developments,

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:41.420
such an economy would
aim to liberate humanity

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:44.160
from the drudgery of
work while simultaneously

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:46.690
producing increasing amounts of wealth."

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:49.380
And this is how automation
can finally help us realize,

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:51.740
economists John Maynard
Keynes' prediction,

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:55.310
that by 2030, we would all
be working 15 hour weeks

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:58.680
or three hour days due to
the efficiency of technology.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:02.489
To the question, but won't
automation ruin all of our jobs?

00:06:02.489 --> 00:06:05.180
Srnicek and Williams
utilize the main lesson

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:07.777
from everyone's first
improv class and say,

00:06:07.777 --> 00:06:11.830
"Yes, and in doing this, it
will create a world where work

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:13.460
is no longer necessary."

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:14.990
According to their argument,

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:17.270
automation doesn't have
to be something that lies

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:19.420
in the hands of a small crew of tech pros

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:21.340
and multinational corporations.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:24.560
Instead, it's something that
should be held in the commons

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:27.150
so that its benefits can be shared by all.

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:28.820
And in making this argument,

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:30.500
theorists also call to question,

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:32.538
the old deeply American assumption

00:06:32.538 --> 00:06:35.710
that work is an essential
feature of human life

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:37.942
and that without it,
we'd all be lazy bastards

00:06:37.942 --> 00:06:40.470
like in "Idiocracy" or "Futurama."

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:43.210
- If I wasn't so lazy, I'd
punch you in the stomach.

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:46.118
- For a quick philosophical
detour on the value of work,

00:06:46.118 --> 00:06:49.000
thinkers as far apart
as Aristotle and Marks,

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.100
have both argued that work is essential

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:52.860
to what it means to be human.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:55.250
And that we can't fully
actualize our humanity

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:57.680
if we're not engaging
in some type of labor.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:06:59.930
So this means that
philosophically speaking,

00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:01.215
it's good to have jobs.

00:07:01.215 --> 00:07:03.400
Right? Not necessarily.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:04.830
When they say work,

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:07.230
they are talking about human consciousness

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:10.410
externalizing itself
by working on the world

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:11.860
outside of itself.

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:14.470
So if you enjoy spending
a day in the park,

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:17.190
working on your drawings
of birds, that's work.

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:19.410
Staying up late to learn how
to play your favorite songs

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:21.213
on the flugelhorn, work.

00:07:21.213 --> 00:07:22.860
Reading a Wikipedia page

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.300
about "The Fall of the Roman Empire,"

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.840
so you can argue that
it's all happening again,

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:29.000
you guessed it, work.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:31.140
So when theorists argue
for the end of work,

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:33.810
they're really arguing
for the end of wage labor.

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:36.930
I.E, the necessity of performing
labor for someone else

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:39.405
so that you can make
enough money to not die.

00:07:39.405 --> 00:07:42.301
Okay, philosophical detour, over.

00:07:42.301 --> 00:07:44.580
While they acknowledge
that a world without work

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:47.750
might be far away, Srnicek
and Williams do think

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:49.610
that we can at least begin to taste a bit

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:51.130
of that good life today,

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:53.230
by shifting towards a four day work week.

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:54.510
This has also been argued

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:56.540
by authors Kyle Lewis and Will Stronge

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:58.537
in theie recent book "Overtime."

00:07:58.537 --> 00:08:00.330
"Why need a shorter work week."

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:03.310
For Lewis and Stronge, shifting
to a four day work week

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:06.340
is not only the obvious and ethical move

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:07.962
in response to increased automation,

00:08:07.962 --> 00:08:10.668
but it actually makes
financial sense as well.

00:08:10.668 --> 00:08:13.700
According to a report by
the Autonomy think tank

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:15.510
of which Stronge and Lewis remembers,

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:17.742
after a trial of a four
day work week in Iceland,

00:08:17.742 --> 00:08:21.970
86% of the country's workforce
are now working shorter hours

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:24.340
or gaining the right
to shorten their hours.

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:25.750
Productivity remained the same

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:28.820
or improved across the
majority of trial workplaces,

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:31.680
and workers well-being
dramatically increased

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.700
across a range of indicators,

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:35.260
from perceived stress and burnout,

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:37.570
to health and work-life balance.

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:39.080
So if these thinkers are right,

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:43.640
then we need is a super no
big deal, gradual revolution

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:46.710
in which the people reappropriate
automated technologies

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:49.100
while developing new
and better technologies,

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:51.310
all at the aim of alleviating labor time,

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:53.580
so that humans can experience
lives of flourishing,

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:56.140
all made possible by robots, algorithms

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:57.795
and a hearty dose of UBI.

00:08:57.795 --> 00:09:01.660
Easy, right? Yeah, not so much.

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:03.490
And it's not just that recent years

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:05.200
have left us less than hopeful

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:07.834
about some type of humanist
political revolution.

00:09:07.834 --> 00:09:11.940
It's also that robots
suck at making burgers.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:14.367
As Gavin Mueller writes
in "Futures of Work,"

00:09:14.367 --> 00:09:18.160
"In March, 2018, Flippy,
a burger-flipping robot,

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:20.300
was rolled out at the Pasadena location

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:22.420
of fast-food chain CaliBurger,

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:25.020
to great fanfare and numerous headlines.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:26.340
The implication was clear.

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:27.720
Would this humble machine

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:29.640
spell the end of the fast-food job,

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:32.633
the metonym for low skilled
entry level occupations?

00:09:32.633 --> 00:09:33.900
Not exactly.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:37.100
In an event that provoked
far less press coverage,

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:40.610
Flippy was retired after one day of work."

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:41.850
And whose fault was this?

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:45.060
According to CaliBurger, it
was the dumb humans of course.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:47.970
They argued that their
workers clumsy fingers

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:51.020
were too slow to dress the
burgers among other tasks,

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:52.197
which as Mueller put it,

00:09:52.197 --> 00:09:55.710
caused Flippy's mini
achievements to pile up.

00:09:55.710 --> 00:09:58.080
Nevermind that some
journalists had already noticed

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.030
that Flippy actually,
wasn't very good at its job.

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:05.226
CaliBurger was ready to
protect Flippy at all costs.

00:10:05.226 --> 00:10:08.200
If the robots can't make fast food burgers

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:12.770
it seems like we're a long
way off from full automation.

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:14.190
And this complicates the narrative

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:16.430
about us all becoming obsolete.

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:19.361
Mueller also discusses the
concept of consumer labor,

00:10:19.361 --> 00:10:21.900
which describes the work we end up doing

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:23.786
when we use things like self checkouts.

00:10:23.786 --> 00:10:26.941
In these scenarios, rather
than abolishing work,

00:10:26.941 --> 00:10:28.900
automation proliferates it.

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:31.710
By isolating tasks and
redistributing them to others

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:33.200
expected to do it for free,

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:35.950
digital technologies
contribute to overwork.

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:38.369
So a lot of what passes for automation

00:10:38.369 --> 00:10:41.860
is really just passing
the buck to consumers.

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:43.821
Think about it, when
we use self checkouts,

00:10:43.821 --> 00:10:47.139
we're just the ones
doing the exact same job

00:10:47.139 --> 00:10:49.760
that a traditional checkout
worker would be doing.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:52.390
Except of course we do it for free.

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:54.410
And things get even more insidious

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:56.880
when we consider
companies like Samasource,

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:00.010
which utilizes cheap labor
in developing countries

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:03.060
to feed repetitive data into
machine learning systems.

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:06.010
These low wage workers
are thus the ghost hidden

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:08.820
in the automated algorithms
that we're meant to believe

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:10.080
are on a fast track

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:12.670
to making human cognitive labor obsolete.

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:15.820
The company's CEO,
Leila Janah, even argues

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:18.350
that paying these workers super low wages

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:20.632
is actually a moral thing to do.

00:11:20.632 --> 00:11:23.610
"If we were to pay people
substantially more than that,

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:25.430
we would throw everything off.

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:27.560
That would have a
potentially negative impact

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:29.750
on the cost of housing
and the cost of food

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:32.265
in the communities, in
which our workers thrive."

00:11:32.265 --> 00:11:35.200
It seems like many of the
automated technologies

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:36.840
of the future, aren't much more

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:38.830
than glorified Theranos machines,

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:41.170
offering the illusion
of futuristic technology

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:44.156
while just obscuring the
types of traditional labor

00:11:44.156 --> 00:11:46.120
making it all possible.

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:49.050
And speaking of things that
automation will never replace,

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:51.990
I can't imagine any robot
ever matching the soulless

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:55.440
and spiritually cavernous
baritone of Elizabeth Holmes.

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:58.490
- At the highest level, we
didn't have the right leadership

00:11:58.490 --> 00:11:59.650
in the laboratory.

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:01.940
- But the skepticism
about the automated future

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:04.800
gets even more, well, skeptical

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:06.300
in the work of Aaron Benanav.

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.395
In his book, "Automation
and the Future of Work,"

00:12:08.395 --> 00:12:10.610
Benanav argues that the narrative

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:13.744
about the radical job losses
sure to be caused by automation

00:12:13.744 --> 00:12:15.960
is largely overblown.

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:18.250
And as the result of
journalists and scholars

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:20.880
over emphasizing the importance
of productivity rates,

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:24.039
i.e, the ratio of labor
outcome to employment

00:12:24.039 --> 00:12:26.560
and under emphasizing output rates.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:29.440
I.E, a measure of the volume of production

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:31.070
in terms of value added.

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:32.810
The narrative around automation has been

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:35.930
that it's driven up productivity
at the expense of workers.

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:38.210
I.E, because we're now so productive,

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:40.350
we don't need workers anymore.

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:42.780
But Benanav notes that in the 2010s,

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:47.200
there was basically 0% growth
in manufacturing productivity.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:49.890
According to this logic,
actual productivity rates

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:52.310
don't support the
narrative about automation

00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:54.150
radically changing the economy.

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:55.537
According to Benanav,

00:12:55.537 --> 00:12:58.610
"No absolute decline in levels
of manufacturing production

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:01.570
has taken place, but
there has been a decline

00:13:01.570 --> 00:13:04.120
in the output growth rate, with the result

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:07.240
that output is growing more
slowly than productivity."

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:09.629
This basically means that
the reason we have fewer jobs

00:13:09.629 --> 00:13:12.308
isn't because of automation
fuel productivity,

00:13:12.308 --> 00:13:15.600
it's because manufacturing
output is declining.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:18.293
And in general, when
output is up, jobs are up,

00:13:18.293 --> 00:13:21.317
and when output declines,
so does job creation.

00:13:21.317 --> 00:13:23.470
Benanav also notes that the countries

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:24.999
with the highest rates of automation,

00:13:24.999 --> 00:13:27.190
have some of the highest employment rates

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:28.530
in the manufacturing sector.

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:31.890
Arguing that, "In the context
of intense global competition,

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:34.100
high degrees of robotization
have given firms

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:37.100
competitive advantages, allowing
them to take market share

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:38.720
from firms in other countries.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.150
Thus Germany, Japan and South Korea

00:13:41.150 --> 00:13:43.475
have some of the highest
levels of robotization.

00:13:43.475 --> 00:13:46.872
They also have the largest
trade surpluses in the world.

00:13:46.872 --> 00:13:49.283
Workers in European and East Asian firms

00:13:49.283 --> 00:13:52.420
know that automation helps
preserve their jobs."

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:54.930
At the same time countries
like the United States

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:57.350
and the United Kingdom,
have both lower rates

00:13:57.350 --> 00:14:00.300
of robotic automation and
fewer workers employed

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:01.450
in manufacturing.

00:14:01.450 --> 00:14:04.180
Basically, we might
actually be more competitive

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:07.120
in manufacturing if we had
higher rates of automation.

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:09.790
But Hey, at least we have baseball,

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:13.120
and the Brits have the Queen,
who definitely isn't dead.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:15.441
So you win some, you lose some.

00:14:15.441 --> 00:14:19.420
Now Benanav isn't arguing that
automation isn't increasing,

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:22.540
but rather that it's already
been increasing for decades.

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:24.950
And it hasn't led to the
radical shifts in society

00:14:24.950 --> 00:14:26.340
that some have predicted.

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:27.677
For that matter he says,

00:14:27.677 --> 00:14:30.250
"What automation theorists
describe as the result

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:34.050
of rising technological dynamism
is actually the consequence

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:36.200
of worsening economic stagnation.

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:38.100
Productivity growth rates appear to rise,

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:41.160
when in reality, output
growth rates are falling."

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:44.620
However, Benanav isn't arguing
that everything is chill

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:46.110
and that the automation theorists

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:48.380
are just a bunch of boys crying wolf,

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:51.970
because even if automation is
not itself the primary cause

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:55.300
of a low demand for labor, it
is nevertheless the case that,

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:58.400
in a slow growing world
economy, technological changes

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:00.030
within a near future horizon

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:03.430
may still threaten large numbers
of jobs with destruction,

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:05.620
in a context of economic stagnation

00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:07.630
and slower rates of job creation.

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:09.200
So while things aren't looking peachy

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:10.560
through his glasses either,

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:13.301
he doesn't think automation
is the sole boogieman.

00:15:13.301 --> 00:15:16.310
And this is where his research
seems to bring us back

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:18.805
to the work of Srnicek and
Williams and Stronge and Lewis,

00:15:18.805 --> 00:15:21.650
all of whom call for us
to utilize the benefits

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:23.700
of increased automated technologies

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:26.300
to decrease our work weeks in labor time.

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:29.460
As we've seen with studies in
Iceland, it's already possible

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:33.170
for people to work less, without
sacrificing productivity,

00:15:33.170 --> 00:15:36.830
and it's making those people
way happier in the process.

00:15:36.830 --> 00:15:38.700
But if automation isn't going to cause

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:40.770
a labor apocalypse tomorrow,

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:43.120
then we should use this
time to push for things

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:44.442
like a four day work week.

00:15:44.442 --> 00:15:47.120
And all in all, it seems like
the two potential futures

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:48.490
offered to us by automation,

00:15:48.490 --> 00:15:51.550
are either a slightly worse
version of the status quo

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:55.310
or a future where we're at
least all working a little less.

00:15:55.310 --> 00:15:57.860
We know we threw a lot of
complicated jargon at you today,

00:15:57.860 --> 00:16:00.736
but worry not, as we have a
couple of resources to help.

00:16:00.736 --> 00:16:02.690
First off, there's an exclusive interview

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:04.940
that our friend Helen
did with Aaron Benanav,

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:06.270
where he explains his critique

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:08.040
of the automation theorist in detail.

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:10.409
So be sure to click the
link in the description

00:16:10.409 --> 00:16:11.760
and check that out.

00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:13.080
And if you wanna hear more about the work

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:16.040
of Stronge and Lewis and their
call for a four day work week

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:17.850
I interviewed them both a few months back,

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:19.610
and you can check out the whole thing

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:21.420
by clicking the link in the description.

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:23.330
And of course, be sure to check out

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:24.608
both of their books as well.

00:16:24.608 --> 00:16:27.690
And maybe the real
takeaway from all of this

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:29.790
is that you are technically doing work

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:31.790
when you use the self
checkout of the grocery store.

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:33.780
So if you happen to enter the code

00:16:33.780 --> 00:16:37.110
for the cheapest fruit or produce
item, it's usually bananas

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:39.847
to buy something more
expensive you've earned it.

00:16:39.847 --> 00:16:41.210
But what do you guys think?

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:44.355
Is automation going to
render human labor obsolete

00:16:44.355 --> 00:16:46.820
or are things a little more hopeful

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:48.770
than the doomsayers are telling us?

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:50.222
Let us know in the comments.

00:16:50.222 --> 00:16:53.211
Huge things to our patrons
